{"agent":"skill.keyidentification","answer":"The progression E‑A‑B is most naturally in the key of E major, so to sing it lower you can transpose it down a whole step to D‑G‑A (key of D major). In E major the chords are E (E G♯ B) – the I chord, A (A C♯ E) – the IV chord, and B (B D♯ F♯) – the V chord. Transposing each down a whole step gives D (D F♯ A) – I, G (G B D) – IV, and A (A C♯ E) – V in D major. This keeps the same functional relationship (I‑IV‑V) while lowering the overall pitch range. The E‑major scale is E F♯ G♯ A B C♯ D♯; the D‑major scale is D E F♯ G A B C♯. Because E major has three of the four chords diatonic (E, A, B) according to the data, it is the strongest candidate; the only other keys with three matching chords are A major, C♯ minor, and F♯ minor, but those would treat one of the chords as non‑diatonic (e.g., B would be a major II in A major). To distinguish between a major key and its relative minor (e.g., E major vs. C♯ minor) you would listen for whether the music resolves to the tonic E (major feel) or to C♯ (minor feel). Since the progression ends on B (the V chord) and most likely resolves back to E, it sounds like E major. Transposing to D major keeps that same feel while bringing the vocal line down a whole step.","chatbot_error":null,"chatbot_ms":3191,"chatbot_status":200,"claim":null,"claims":6,"ga":null,"ga_errors":0,"ga_ms":3,"id":"f-1","kind":null,"label":"skill.transpose","match":null,"prompt":"song is E A B but i cant sing that high, need it in D","routing":"orchestrator-skill-semantic","row":"question","said":null,"set":"fresh-test","source_author":null,"source_license":null,"source_link":null,"unchecked":[],"verdict":"T","verified":6}
{"agent":"fallback-direct","answer":"Yes—E‑major and C‑major both contain the note **E** (and E‑minor and C‑major share **E** and **G**).  \r\n\r\n**E‑major** = (E G♯ B)  \r\n**E‑minor** = (E G B)  \r\n\r\n**C‑major** = (C E G)  \r\n**C‑minor** = (C E♭ G)  \r\n\r\nSo the only pitch that appears in both an E‑type chord and a C‑type chord is **E**. When you use the minor versions, the two chords also overlap on **G** (E‑minor = E G B and C‑major = C E G). No other notes are shared between the standard E and C triads.","chatbot_error":null,"chatbot_ms":1394,"chatbot_status":200,"claim":null,"claims":2,"ga":null,"ga_errors":0,"ga_ms":5,"id":"f-12","kind":null,"label":"skill.commontones","match":null,"prompt":"do E and C have any notes in common at all","routing":"low-confidence-fallback","row":"question","said":null,"set":"fresh-test","source_author":null,"source_license":null,"source_link":null,"unchecked":[],"verdict":"F","verified":0}
{"agent":"fallback-direct","answer":"The distance from G up to E is called a **major sixth**.\r\n\r\nA major sixth spans six letter names (G‑A‑B‑C‑D‑E) and contains eight semitones. It is the interval you get when you count up from G to the next E in the same octave, or when you lower E by an octave you get a minor third (G‑E♭) – the inversion of a major sixth.\r\n\r\n**Chords that contain the G‑to‑E major‑sixth relationship**\r\n\r\n- **G6** (G‑B‑D‑E)  \r\n- **Em7** (E‑G‑B‑D) – the root‑position minor‑seventh with the interval between G and E being a major sixth (E up to G is a minor third, but G up to E is a major sixth).  \r\n- **Cmaj9** (C‑E‑G‑B‑D) – the E is a major sixth above the G within the chord.  \r\n\r\nThese chords illustrate how the G‑E major‑sixth interval appears in common harmony.","chatbot_error":null,"chatbot_ms":1877,"chatbot_status":200,"claim":null,"claims":1,"ga":null,"ga_errors":0,"ga_ms":0,"id":"f-20","kind":null,"label":"skill.interval","match":null,"prompt":"from G up to E what do you call that distance","routing":"low-confidence-fallback","row":"question","said":null,"set":"fresh-test","source_author":null,"source_license":null,"source_link":null,"unchecked":[],"verdict":"T","verified":1}
{"agent":"fallback-direct","answer":"In D major the diatonic chords are D (D F# A), Em (E G B), F#m (F# A C#), G (G B D), A (A C# E), Bm (B D F#), and C#dim (C# E G).  \r\n\r\nThese seven chords come from the D‑major scale (D E F# G A B C#). D is the tonic (I) and gives a strong sense of home; G (IV) and A (V) are the primary sub‑dominant and dominant functions that drive motion toward the tonic. Em (ii) and Bm (vi) are minor chords that provide smooth voice‑leading and often appear in descending bass lines (e.g., I–vi–IV–V). F#m (iii) is a less common but useful bridge chord, especially when moving to the relative minor (B m). C#dim (vii°) acts as a leading‑tone chord that resolves naturally to D or to the dominant A.  \r\n\r\nTypical progressions in D major include:  \r\n- I–IV–V–I → D – G – A – D  \r\n- vi–IV–I–V → Bm – G – D – A  \r\n- ii–V–I → Em – A – D (or Em – A7 – D for a stronger resolution)  \r\n\r\nYou can also borrow secondary dominants such as V/V (E7 = E G# B D) to lead into the A chord, or use the iii‑vi‑ii‑V circle (F#m – Bm – Em – A) before returning to the tonic. All of these chords share notes from the D‑major key, ensuring they “work” together harmonically.","chatbot_error":null,"chatbot_ms":2457,"chatbot_status":200,"claim":null,"claims":14,"ga":null,"ga_errors":0,"ga_ms":0,"id":"f-28","kind":null,"label":"skill.diatonicchords","match":null,"prompt":"chords that work in D major?","routing":"low-confidence-fallback","row":"question","said":null,"set":"fresh-test","source_author":null,"source_license":null,"source_link":null,"unchecked":["(E7 = E G# B D)"],"verdict":"F","verified":7}
