{"agent":"skill.keyidentification","answer":"The progression transposed up a whole step is Bm (B D F#) – G (G B D) – D (D F# A) – A (A C# E). To move each chord a whole step (two semitones) you raise every root note by that interval: A → B, F → G, C → D, G → A. The new minor chord Bm keeps the minor quality (B‑D‑F#) and each major chord keeps its major triad (G‑B‑D, D‑F#‑A, A‑C#‑E). You can play the transposed sequence just like the original, using the same shapes moved up two frets on the neck or using a capo at the 2nd fret and playing the original shapes.","chatbot_error":null,"chatbot_ms":3427,"chatbot_status":200,"claim":null,"claims":5,"ga":null,"ga_errors":0,"ga_ms":2,"id":"f-73","kind":null,"label":"skill.transpose","match":null,"prompt":"put Am F C G up a whole step pls","routing":"orchestrator-skill-semantic","row":"question","said":null,"set":"fresh-test","source_author":null,"source_license":null,"source_link":null,"unchecked":[],"verdict":"F","verified":4}
{"agent":"skill.scaleinfo","answer":"The A minor scale has 7 notes: **A – B – C – D – E – F – G**. Relative major: C major.","chatbot_error":null,"chatbot_ms":30,"chatbot_status":200,"claim":null,"claims":1,"ga":null,"ga_errors":0,"ga_ms":0,"id":"f-75","kind":null,"label":"skill.interval","match":null,"prompt":"C up to Ab, is that a minor 6th or an aug 5th, how do u tell","routing":"orchestrator-skill-semantic","row":"question","said":null,"set":"fresh-test","source_author":null,"source_license":null,"source_link":null,"unchecked":[],"verdict":"F","verified":0}
{"agent":"fallback-direct","answer":"It is a D‑minor triad with an augmented fifth, notated **Dm(♯5) (D–F–A♯)**.  \r\n\r\n**Why:** The chord contains the root D, the minor third F, and an altered fifth A♯ (the enharmonic equivalent of B♭). This alteration raises the perfect fifth (A) a semitone, giving the chord its “♯5” label.  \r\n\r\n**Typical harmonic function:**  \r\n- **Chromatic passing/voice‑leading chord:** In many tonal contexts Dm(♯5) appears as a brief, coloristic chord that leads smoothly to a diatonic chord a half step away, such as an E‑minor (vi) or G‑major (V) chord.  \r\n- **Secondary dominant alteration:** It can be heard as an altered version of V/vi (the dominant of the vi chord), where the usual D‑F♯‑A (D7) is modified by lowering the third (F♯ → F) and raising the fifth (A → A♯). This creates a strong pull toward the vi chord.  \r\n- **German‑augmented‑sixth reinterpretation:** When respelled enharmonically (D–F–B♭), the chord resembles a German augmented‑sixth (♭III+ in C major) that resolves to the dominant (G).  \r\n\r\nThus, D‑F‑A♯ is best identified as **Dm(♯5)**, serving mainly as a chromatic, tension‑building chord that resolves to a more stable tonic, subdominant, or dominant harmony depending on the surrounding progression.","chatbot_error":null,"chatbot_ms":4758,"chatbot_status":200,"claim":null,"claims":1,"ga":null,"ga_errors":0,"ga_ms":0,"id":"m-37","kind":null,"label":"ambiguous","match":null,"prompt":"What is the name and function of the D-F-A♯ chord in this guitar score?","routing":"low-confidence-fallback","row":"question","said":null,"set":"music-se","source_author":"Tikhon","source_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","source_link":"https://music.stackexchange.com/questions/144196/what-is-the-name-and-function-of-the-d-f-a%e2%99%af-chord-in-this-guitar-score","unchecked":[],"verdict":"F","verified":0}
